(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-antibacterials-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Antibacterials Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 1","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which penicillin dosage form is given intramuscularly as a single long-acting dose?","choices":["Penicillin G (benzylpenicillin)","Ampicillin sodium","Penicillin G benzathine (Bicillin)","Penicillin V potassium (Pen VK)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The benzathine salt is the depot form, which is why one injection can cover an indication such as syphilis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which antibiotic classes bind the 50S ribosomal subunit, and which bind the 30S?","choices":["50S — vancomycin, daptomycin; 30S — linezolid, tigecycline","50S: macrolides, clindamycin. 30S: aminoglycosides, tetracyclines","50S: aminoglycosides, tetracyclines. 30S: macrolides, clindamycin","50S — fluoroquinolones, metronidazole; 30S — penicillins, cephalosporins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The bacterial ribosome is 70S against the mammalian 80S, which is the basis for selectivity. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"For a time-dependent agent with no post-antibiotic effect, what dosing strategy does the lecture recommend?","choices":["Alternate-day dosing to allow tissue concentrations to accumulate between doses","Frequent infusions, level up 40 to 70% of the interval","A single large daily dose, taking advantage of the prolonged post-antibiotic effect","A loading dose, then widely spaced doses by trough alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Without a post-antibiotic effect, organisms resume growing as soon as the level falls, so time above the threshold is what matters. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How do levofloxacin and moxifloxacin differ in coverage?","choices":["Moxifloxacin requires renal adjustment; levofloxacin does not","Levofloxacin has Pseudomonas coverage; moxifloxacin does not","Moxifloxacin has Pseudomonas coverage; levofloxacin does not","Levofloxacin covers atypicals; moxifloxacin does not"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both improve on ciprofloxacin's Gram-positive activity and both cover community-acquired pneumonia. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Which atypical organisms do macrolides cover?","choices":["Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter, Burkholderia, Stenotrophomonas","Bacteroides fragilis and Clostridium perfringens","Rickettsiae, Borrelia burgdorferi and Brucella","Legionella, Mycoplasma, Chlamydophila, Chlamydia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and this atypical coverage is what makes them a community-acquired pneumonia agent. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Why did aminoglycoside dosing move from every 8 hours to every 24 hours?","choices":["Once-daily dosing eliminates the need for therapeutic drug monitoring","Renal clearance is so rapid that more frequent dosing has no effect","Post-antibiotic effect and concentration-dependent killing","Time-dependent killing over a longer interval"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This is the clearest clinical application of the concentration-dependent pattern from earlier in the lecture. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What is the defining clinical advantage of aztreonam?","choices":["No beta-lactam cross-reactivity; safe in true allergy","The broadest beta-lactam spectrum, including anaerobes","Oral bioavailability allowing outpatient completion of therapy","Reliable central nervous system penetration for meningitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That property is what makes a monobactam worth having despite its narrow Gram-negative-only spectrum. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is daptomycin's mechanism, and what is its single most important limitation?","choices":["Inhibits the 50S subunit; unusable in renal failure","It inhibits cell wall cross-linking, and it cannot be used in penicillin allergy","It inhibits DNA gyrase, and it cannot be used under 18 years of age","Depolarizes the membrane; cannot be used in pneumonia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Daptomycin is inactivated by pulmonary surfactant, so the lung is the one site where it fails. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which tetracycline precautions relate to children and pregnancy?","choices":["Teeth discoloration and skeletal effects; avoid under 8","Cartilage damage — avoid under 18 years old","Kernicterus; avoid in the first 30 days of life","Grey baby syndrome — avoid in all trimesters"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both follow from the drug binding calcium in developing bone and teeth. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which adverse reactions does the lecture list for the antipseudomonal penicillins?","choices":["Hypersensitivity reaction, rash, and diarrhoea","Ototoxicity, nephrotoxicity, and red man syndrome","Tendon rupture, peripheral neuropathy, and central nervous system toxicity","Disulfiram-like reaction with ethanol and metallic taste"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with monitoring for anaphylaxis, renal function, complete blood count and bleeding. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What monitoring does the lecture specify for daptomycin?","choices":["Muscle pain and creatine phosphokinase levels","Platelet count, because of thrombocytopenia","Trough concentrations drawn before the fourth dose","Liver function tests and bilirubin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The muscle toxicity is what the monitoring is aimed at. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which indications are given for cefazolin?","choices":["Surgical prophylaxis, MSSA and urinary tract infection","Neutropenic fever and hospital-acquired pneumonia","Meningitis and neonatal sepsis","Complicated intra-abdominal and anaerobic infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cephalexin covers a similar niche orally, aimed at cellulitis and urinary tract infection. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Ampicillin and amoxicillin are the drugs of choice for which set of indications?","choices":["Syphilis, gas gangrene, and meningococcal disease","Enterococcus, Listeria, endocarditis prophylaxis and pneumonia","Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia and osteomyelitis","Pseudomonal, nosocomial and polymicrobial infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aminopenicillins add some Gram-negative aerobic activity to the natural penicillin spectrum but still lack Staphylococcus coverage. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A patient taking an aminopenicillin is also on warfarin. What does the lecture identify as the interaction?","choices":["No clinically meaningful interaction, since penicillins are renally cleared","Increased risk of tendon rupture when the two are combined","Abnormal prolongation of the prothrombin time","Reduced anticoagulant effect requiring an increased warfarin dose"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists anticoagulants first among the aminopenicillin interactions. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Clindamycin is the drug of choice for which situations?","choices":["Meningitis, cerebral abscess and neurosurgical prophylaxis","Toxin-mediated disease, skin, bone, penicillin allergy","Pseudomonal pneumonia and febrile neutropenia","Urinary tract infection and pyelonephritis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The toxin-mediated indication follows from inhibiting bacterial protein synthesis and therefore toxin production. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What distinguishes a bacteriostatic drug from a bactericidal drug, as the lecture defines them?","choices":["Static needs a higher concentration than cidal","Bacteriostatic agents are given orally and bactericidal agents are given intravenously","Static halts growth; cidal disrupts enough to kill","Static is Gram-positive only; cidal covers both"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture's split is about whether the drug itself kills, not about how fast it works. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What monitoring does the lecture specify for the penicillinase-resistant penicillins?","choices":["Serum drug troughs drawn before the fourth dose","Creatine phosphokinase and muscle pain","Hepatic function, complete blood count, and signs or symptoms of anaphylaxis","Renal function alone, since the class is cleared entirely by the kidney"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Note the dosing consideration for this class is hepatic rather than renal function, unlike most other penicillins. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"How do sulfamethoxazole and trimethoprim block folate synthesis at two different steps?","choices":["Sulfa at the reductase, trimethoprim at the PABA step","Both inhibit dihydrofolate reductase, at different binding sites","Sulfa blocks gut folate; trimethoprim blocks reabsorption","Sulfa at the PABA step, trimethoprim at the reductase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Two sequential steps in one pathway, which is what makes the combination synergistic. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"What is the approximate rate of penicillin cross-sensitivity with cephalosporins in this lecture?","choices":["Cross-sensitivity does not occur at all between the two classes","Less than 1 percent","Approximately 10 percent","Approximately 25 percent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The figure matters clinically because a reported penicillin allergy does not automatically exclude a cephalosporin. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Under drug factors for choosing a regimen, which toxicities does the lecture list as the organ systems to consider?","choices":["Cardiac, pulmonary, endocrine, musculoskeletal, ocular, and dermatologic systems","Gastrointestinal, renal, hepatic, ocular, reproductive, immunologic","Hematologic, renal, hepatic, endocrine, vascular, lymphatic","Gastrointestinal, integument, hematologic, central nervous system, hepatic, renal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Six systems, and they map onto the monitoring parameters repeated for individual classes later in the lecture. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Why is polymyxin resistance uncommon, and why is use expected to increase?","choices":["Bacteria cannot circumvent it and the toxicity is gone","It is used only topically, so systemic resistance cannot emerge","It is always combined with a beta-lactamase inhibitor that prevents resistance","Unused for 50 years; resistance is bringing it back"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Low historical exposure is the reason resistance never developed widely. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Cefepime is the drug of choice for which infections?","choices":["Surgical prophylaxis and uncomplicated cystitis","Neutropenic fever, nosocomial pneumonia and Pseudomonas","Community-acquired pneumonia and skin infection","Complicated intra-abdominal and complicated urinary tract infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It has Gram-negative and antipseudomonal activity plus retained Gram-positive activity, though no methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, Enterococcus or anaerobic coverage. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What must a patient starting metronidazole be counselled to avoid, and why?","choices":["Tyramine-containing foods, because of hypertensive crisis","Sun exposure, because of photosensitivity","Ethanol, because of a disulfiram-like reaction","Dairy products, because of chelation reducing absorption"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This is the class's signature counselling point, alongside headache, nausea and vomiting. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which organism has increasingly rendered the penicillinase-resistant penicillins ineffective, and what replaces them?","choices":["MRSA, for which vancomycin is the treatment of choice","Vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus, for which linezolid is the treatment of choice","Extended-spectrum beta-lactamase producing organisms, for which carbapenems are preferred","Pseudomonas aeruginosa, for which cefepime is preferred"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The class was built for Staphylococcus aureus and methicillin resistance is precisely what defeats it. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which linezolid interactions can produce serotonin syndrome?","choices":["Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, tyramine-containing foods, and pseudoephedrine","Warfarin, phenytoin and digoxin","Iron, calcium and dairy products","Ethanol, which produces a disulfiram-like reaction with flushing and vomiting"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and platelets are monitored separately because of thrombocytopenia. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which carbapenem lacks Pseudomonas aeruginosa coverage?","choices":["Meropenem","Imipenem","Doripenem","Ertapenem"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The class covers Pseudomonas as a rule, and ertapenem is the stated exception. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"How do macrolides interact with other drugs, and in what order of severity?","choices":["They displace substrates from plasma protein binding sites","Metabolites inactivate CYP3A, raising substrate levels","They induce CYP3A, lowering substrate levels","They chelate divalent cations, reducing absorption of co-administered drugs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The ranking is the practical point: azithromycin is the one to reach for when interactions matter. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which toxicities define the aminoglycoside class?","choices":["Hepatotoxicity and thrombocytopenia","Tendon rupture and peripheral neuropathy","Serotonin syndrome, hypertensive crisis, and thrombocytopenia","Renal toxicity and ototoxicity, requiring renal dose adjustment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and vancomycin shares both, which is why the combination is watched carefully. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"How does vancomycin inhibit cell wall synthesis, and how does this differ from a penicillin?","choices":["Binds the D-alanine residues to block cross-linking","Covalently inactivates the penicillin-binding protein","It inhibits the 50S ribosomal subunit and blocks transpeptidation","It disrupts the outer membrane by displacing magnesium and calcium ions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Penicillin mimics the substrate and disables the enzyme; vancomycin covers the substrate itself, which is why resistance is harder to develop. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which cephalosporin provides methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus coverage?","choices":["Ceftolozane/tazobactam, a fifth generation agent","Ceftriaxone, a third generation agent","Ceftaroline, a fifth generation agent","Cefepime, a fourth generation agent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is the cephalosporin that breaks the class's usual absence of methicillin-resistant coverage, and it must be renally adjusted. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 37"}]};
